= Knowledge graph — Technical =

== Graph model ==
A labeled knowledge graph can be represented as triples:

<pre>
G ⊆ E × R × (E ∪ L)
</pre>

where:
* E = entities/resources;
* R = relation/property types;
* L = literals.

More elaborate definitions add schema, qualifiers, provenance or temporal dimensions.

== Schema ==
A schema may define:

<pre>
class hierarchy
property hierarchy
domain/range
constraints
identity rules
</pre>

Schema semantics determine which derived edges are valid.

== Deductive closure ==
Given graph G and rule/ontology O:

<pre>
closure_O(G)
</pre>

contains facts entailed by G under O.

Materializing closure trades storage for query speed.

Query-time reasoning trades latency for less duplication.

== Inductive models ==
Embedding approaches map graph elements to vectors:

<pre>
e(entity) ∈ R^d
r(relation) ∈ R^k
</pre>

and learn scoring functions over triples.

They support completion and ranking but do not provide classical entailment guarantees.

== Property graph versus RDF ==
Property graphs typically allow properties on nodes/edges directly.

RDF uses triples and graph/dataset semantics.

Both can represent similar applications, but identity, typing, reification and query semantics differ.

== Qualifiers ==
Facts such as:

<pre>
Alice worksAt Company from 2020 to 2024
</pre>

need qualifiers.

Patterns include:
* statement nodes;
* RDF 1.2 triple terms;
* named graphs;
* n-ary relations;
* edge properties.

Model choice affects provenance and reasoning.

== Quality dimensions ==
Knowledge-graph quality can include:
* accuracy;
* completeness;
* consistency;
* timeliness;
* provenance;
* entity resolution;
* schema conformance;
* logical satisfiability.

These are distinct axes.

